      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, the knot clusters and moisture things make sense. i'll dig deeper into those.\n\nbut there's one more thing that's really bothering me. i was inspecting one of the beams late last night, and i swear i saw something in the grain—not a knot, but like a small, intentional-looking indentation, almost like a tooth mark or a tiny gouge that wasn't there during the day. it's in a spot where the stress is heaviest. it's weirdly localized and just feels... wrong. if we've ruled out knots and moisture, is there any biological or physical reason why a beam would suddenly show these kinds of tiny, isolated, physical surface marks right where the load is highest?",
      "source": "evaluator"
